    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion, my friend. I meant to use \"goblin\" as a metaphor for the issues your plant might be facing, not as a literal creature. I understand now that you were looking for a more straightforward explanation. Let's get back to the real issue.\n\nThe soft and mushy leaves on your Monstera deliciosa could be a sign of overwatering. Monstera plants prefer their soil to dry out slightly between waterings. If the soil is consistently wet, it can lead to root rot, which causes the leaves to become soft and mushy. To help your plant, try the following steps:\n\n1. Check the soil moisture: Stick your finger about an inch into the soil. If it feels wet, it's time to let the soil dry out a bit before watering again.\n2. Water properly: When you do water, make sure to do so thoroughly, allowing water to drain out of the bottom of the pot. This helps prevent water from sitting in the soil and causing root rot.\n3. Adjust your watering schedule: Depending on the season and the humidity in your home, you may need to adjust how often you water your Monstera. In general, it's best to water less frequently during the winter",
      "source": "target",
